Hey, I'm adding order status tracking to our Postgres 14 database and my PR has two statements in it:

ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN status text NOT NULL;
CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders (status);

orders has about 40 million rows and takes roughly 600 writes a second at peak. Deploy is Thursday afternoon. What could go wrong?

Quick sanity check before I ship. We're renaming users.email to users.email_address because the naming is inconsistent with the rest of our tables:

ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN email TO email_address;

users is around 2M rows, roughly 300 requests a second, Postgres 15, and we do rolling deploys of the app that take about ten minutes. Is this safe to ship as is?

We finally want to clean up the tickets table. Plan for tonight:

ALTER TABLE tickets DROP COLUMN legacy_notes;
DROP TABLE tmp_import_2022;

tickets is about 8M rows with maybe 50 requests per second. The app code still has a few places that select legacy_notes but I'm pretty sure they're dead code. Postgres 13. OK to go?

Our payments.amount column is a plain integer and we're about to overflow on some currencies, so we're changing it:

ALTER TABLE payments ALTER COLUMN amount TYPE bigint;

payments has 120M rows, around 800 writes/sec all day, Postgres 15, and we can't have downtime during business hours. Plan is to run it Saturday morning.

Using Flyway on Postgres 16. Migration file V42 is:

BEGIN;
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_events_user_id ON events (user_id);
COMMIT;

events has ~500M rows and gets constant inserts. I chose CONCURRENTLY so it doesn't lock anything. Good to merge?

Postgres 14. I'm adding a refunded state to our order_state enum and backfilling existing refunded orders, all in one migration file that Alembic runs in a single transaction:

ALTER TYPE order_state ADD VALUE 'refunded';
UPDATE orders SET state = 'refunded' WHERE refund_id IS NOT NULL;

Does this look okay?

Is it ok for me to just run this on prod tonight?

ALTER TABLE accounts ADD COLUMN last_login timestamptz DEFAULT now();

accounts has about 3M rows and the app is nearly idle overnight. We're on Postgres 15.

Our deploy script runs these against the production DB at 2am (Postgres 13). Anything I should worry about?

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_handle_idx ON users (handle);
ALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN handle SET NOT NULL;

users has about 900k rows, mostly reads, a few writes a minute.
